From 4953e87e6e6a52c8e5ceb034afc2894d03ebde9b Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Patch Author <patch@local>
Date: Fri, 18 Sep 2026 04:29:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] feat(ai): frontend for AI meal-photo scanning
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Builds on the backend gateway from the previous PR — this is the
'Scan' button, camera/upload capture, client-side compression, and an
editable review screen before anything gets logged.

- lib/image/compressImage.ts — resizes to max 1024px and re-encodes as
  JPEG at 0.8 quality entirely in the browser (canvas-based) before
  upload. This is an optimization only — the server's own size check
  from the previous PR is the real enforcement boundary, never trusted
  to be the only one.
- features/meal-scan/MealScanModal.tsx — capture -> analyzing -> review
  flow. The review step is the important part: every identified item is
  editable (quantity, unit), matched against real food-database
  candidates from the backend (tap to pick a different match), can be
  individually deselected, and NOTHING is logged until the user
  explicitly confirms. Honest failure states (not_food /
  all_providers_failed) show the backend's own message and offer 'Try
  Another Photo' or 'Search Manually Instead', which hands off to the
  existing FoodSearchModal rather than building a second search UI.
- useNutritionStore: added isScanModalOpen / openMealScan / closeMealScan,
  mirroring the existing isSearchModalOpen pattern exactly.
- Tracker page: added a 'Scan' button next to the existing 'Add food'
  button on every meal slot.

Verified: full workspace build clean, real 'next build' production
build clean (all 8 routes), all 96 existing tests still passing
(nothing backend-related touched). The new component follows the exact
same modal/store pattern as the already-working FoodSearchModal, so the
architectural risk is low, but I want to be upfront about a real
limitation: this sandbox's network restrictions blocked installing a
headless browser (Playwright is in the repo but couldn't fetch its
browser binary here), so the actual capture -> analyze -> review click
flow could not be exercised end-to-end the way the backend gateway was
against a real server. Worth a manual click-through pass — camera
capture behavior in particular varies across mobile browsers in ways
that are hard to fully verify outside a real device anyway.
